lesson g6-proportion-as-an-equation v=1 format=worked grade=6 unit=6 subject=maths levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Proportion as an equation"
  goal "Write two equal ratios as a proportion and find its missing term from the products of the outer and inner terms."

  example {
    worked proportion.missing-term t=0 m=2 n=3 pos=3 level=medium
    say """
      Two equal ratios make a proportion: 4 : 6 = 6 : x. The terms at the ends, 4 and x, are the
      outer terms, and 6 and 6 are the inner terms. In a true proportion the outer terms multiply to
      the same number as the inner terms, so 4 × x = 6 × 6 = 36, and x = 36 ÷ 4 = 9. Check it: 4 : 6
      and 6 : 9 both have the ratio value 2/3. At the mill the grain and the flour stay in one
      ratio, so any question about them can be written this way.
      """
  }
  exercises {
    show proportion.missing-term t=1 m=1 n=3 pos=3 easy-t=0 easy-m=1 easy-n=4 easy-pos=3 hard-t=3 hard-m=2 hard-n=5 hard-pos=0
    show proportion.true-or-not t=2 easy-t=0 hard-t=5
    show proportion.mill-flour s=1 q=0 easy-s=0 easy-q=0 hard-s=3 hard-q=0
    level easy {
      worked proportion.missing-term t=4 m=1 n=3 pos=2 level=medium
    }
    show proportion.missing-term t=2 m=2 n=5 pos=1 easy-t=3 easy-m=1 easy-n=2 easy-pos=2 hard-t=4 hard-m=3 hard-n=4 hard-pos=1
    show proportion.which-equation s=1 o=1 easy-s=0 easy-o=0 hard-s=3 hard-o=2
    show proportion.true-or-not t=3 easy-t=1 hard-t=4
    show proportion.plan-wheel s=2 easy-s=0 hard-s=3
    show proportion.decimal-term s=2 easy-s=0 hard-s=4
    level hard {
      show proportion.mill-flour s=4 q=1
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show proportion.add-mistake s=1 easy-s=0 hard-s=3
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show proportion.same-number s=2 easy-s=0 hard-s=5
  }
  remember {
    say "In a proportion a : b = c : d, the outer terms multiply to the same as the inner terms: a × d = b × c."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is one rule: two ratios are equal exactly when the outer terms multiply to the same
    number as the inner terms. It finds a missing term wherever it sits, including when no whole
    number of times takes one ratio to the other, which is where the tables of the ratio lesson
    stop. The common slip is to keep the difference instead of the ratio (4 : 6 = 10 : 12), so ask
    for the check by products after every answer. In the setting-up question each ratio has to be
    read the same way round, grain to flour on both sides; a flour answer bigger than the grain is
    the sign it was turned. In the two-star question Ella's answer fails the check, and the product
    rule gives the right one. In the three-star question the same number is both inner terms, so
    it times itself makes the product of the outer ones. If the outer and inner terms still get
    mixed, stay here another day and circle them in two colours. Off the page, take a recipe for
    four and write the proportion that scales it for six. This lesson meets Russia's grade 6
    programme, «Отношение. Деление в данном отношении. Масштаб, пропорция. Применение пропорций при
    решении задач.»
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level a second proportion is worked in the teacher's pen, the missing term is the third or fourth and one ratio is a whole number of times the other, the proportions to test are the plainest, the grain scales by three, the plan scale is 2 cm to 5 m, the decimal term is 1.5, Ella's mistake is with 4 : 6, and the same number twice is 4."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the missing term is the first or second and no whole number takes one ratio to the other, the flour comes out as a half and a second grain question runs backwards, the proportions to test are close, the plan scale is 4 cm to 10 m, the decimal term is 0.6 : 1.5, Ella works with 9 : 15, and the same number twice is 12."
  }
}
